{
  "dom_id": "step-q335387",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A brick of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the brick's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the brick is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the brick are the weight due to gravity and drag.",
  "worked_solution": "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the brick:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the brick, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =3g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the brick (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{20}{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the brick is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=3g-\\frac{1}{20}{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the brick, we have\n\n$$\n3g-\\frac{1}{20}{v}^{2}=3\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{1}{60}{v}^{2}.\n$$",
